ifice of the million  plus  who fought and died for our country  oh absolutely i do  i think the american people are  incredibly generous and  uh very appreciative of our military uh  they  uh i i think they absolutely uh are  thankful for the men and women who  served in our in the cloth of the nation  from the revolutionary war into today i  think the american people are very  appreciative of their military  i want to ask you some questions about  today's news and obviously there's a lot  of focus on ukraine and the one question  i get time and time again is  how does this end  i think right now it's a bit early to  actually tell and now you've got a very  significant operational fight going on  in the donbass between the ukrainian  and the russian militaries  how that shapes up in the next few weeks  will probably in large part shape the  outcomes  of what will happen it could end up as a  grind and go on and on and a stalemate  it could end up with one side or the  other  having a decisive victory it could end  up in a peace negotiation there's many  many alternatives and we're not really  sure right now it's up to ukrainians you  said at a news conference last week the  end state's defined by the political  leadership in in in this case president  valencia is going to define the end  state inside the boundaries of ukraine  so does that mean you're going to  support if he wants to let's say take  back crimea from the russians we the  military will support whatever you know  president biden in our political  leadership determines uh but in large  respect i mean this is a war between  ukraine and russia how this ends on the  battlefield is going to be determined by  president salinsky and the  in president putin if like for example  the ukrainians decide to use u.s  provided weapons against the russians in  russian territory i know there's a  policy thing but is that a problem for  us  well i think there's always a  possibility of escalation and we have to  closely manage escalation and the  chances of escalation so we do that and  that's why you saw secretary austin talk  to his counterpart that's why you saw me  talk to my counterpart in order to  manage escalation and prevent this thing  from expanding into something much more  horrific than it already is do you think  putin needs an off-ramp or  are you concerned that humiliating him  would cause some kind of catastrophic  result whether putin has an offer up or  not and whether zielinski has an offer  but not  those are up to those two leaders to  determine they've lost well over a  hundred thousand casualties killed and  wounded on both sides  there's been seven million refugees six  million uh internally displaced persons  horrific damage to the infrastructure of  ukraine in only 90 days this is a very  very serious war that's taken place with  high consumptions of of resources on  both sides  so i think a negotiated outcome is a  logical choice but both sides have to  come to that conclusion on their own we  mentioned section 60 and the veterans  from iraq and afghanistan buried here  looking back through the prison of  history to that time do you think that  we as a country got bogged down in iraq  and afghanistan when really the bigger  threat was china and his china  well there's no question that we you  know fought for 20 years in iraq and  afghanistan again a kind of insurgency  threat  against guerrillas and terrorists and in  in some ways those fights are still  going on terrorism hasn't gone away from  the face of the earth but at the same  time in parallel to those while we were  in iraq and afghanistan china was  building a world-class military and  russia was reforming itself and  preparing for the invasion that they're  executing right now  so bogged down sure i think that we were  clearly engaged and decisively engaged  in both of those conflicts while russia  and china were moving in a different  direction in that region north korea  sable rattling again firing three  missiles last week  talking very aggressively do you  consider that a serious threat as of  today sure north korea is very much a  serious threat they've got significant  capability they've got a very large land  army they've got a capable  missile and nuclear force and that is of  concern to us that's why we have 28 500  troops on the canary peninsula right now  today of course we have a  treaty a defense pact with south korea  and with japan will the regime last  forever no no regime lasts forever so at  some point  my thought is that  korea will once again be a unified  country at some point in the future how  far how it happens when it happens who  knows is the military in your mind ready  for the future  we're moving in that direction i think  that we've got a lot of work to do we're  in a  period of what i call a change in the  character of war where we have uh  fundamental changes in technology for  example robotics and artificial  intelligence this  last series of strategies have been put  out in the last few years are pointing  us in the direction  to adjust and reform our military the  country's divided but it seems the  military is grounding you know there's a  unifying factor with the u.s military do  you see it that way in a divided country  absolutely i mean you're sitting right  here on hallowed ground this is what  unifies us it's those that came before  us and this is where we come from and  we're never going to turn our backs on  the constitution we're not going to turn  our backs on those that are buried here  in arlington and that's what you think  about when you come here absolutely  every time  general thanks for the time great thanks  hey sean hannity here hey click here to  subscribe to fox news youtube page and  catch our hottest interviews and most  compelling analysis you will not get it  anywhere else
 

